Refusing the withdrawal in front of you, rather than the one after it.

  node scripts/firsttransaction.mjs

Studionet, 2026-09-04. Transcript below with the rate limit noise taken out and
nothing else changed; the run was interrupted twice by Studionet's thirty a
minute and five hundred an hour, and the last withdrawal was sent again once the
budget came back.

  guardian 0x5D9D81dDDddA356c1b6E3854C897c37055E5450a
  vault    0x1c9127927A0f732C5D82765d0c23F3952d1D76d1

  protect  {"ok":true,"bounty":"10000000000000000","state":"OPEN"}
  deposit  {"ok":true,"balance":"40000000000000000"}
  floor    {"ok":true,"floor":"not more than 50 percent inside 600 seconds"}

The floor is published after the deposit, so the high point is the funded
balance rather than an empty contract.

taking a quarter, which the floor allows

  {"ok":true,"withdrew":"10000000000000000","left":"30000000000000000"}

taking enough to cross it, in one transaction

  {"ok":false,"halted":false,"blocked_so_far":1,
   "error":"this would cross the floor this contract published"}

Sent again after the rate limit cleared, to be sure the first was not an accident
of timing:

  {"ok":false,"halted":false,"blocked_so_far":2,
   "error":"this would cross the floor this contract published"}
  tx  0xa07d998e3b8476a6a67125fdb23531e3a2bbae656811fb8b543e39d5a3ca5669
  39s, which is what an ordinary transaction costs on this network

What the guard was asked, and answered, as a view inside that same transaction:

  {"ok": true, "would_break": true,
   "held": "30000000000000000", "taking": "15000000000000000",
   "measured_against": "40000000000000000",
   "would_have_fallen": 62, "floor_percent": 50}

Three things worth reading twice.

`"halted": false`. The guard was never raised. Nobody staked a deposit, nobody
raised an alarm, no validator was asked anything, and the protocol is still open
for business on everything that does not cross its own published floor. The
payment was refused and that was the whole of it.

`measured_against` is the high point rather than the current balance. The quarter
that already left counts towards the same window, which is what makes this a
limit on a rate rather than a limit on one transaction. An attacker splitting the
withdrawal in two is refused on the second half.

And `62` against `50` is arithmetic. No prompt, no round, no model. That is why
it can sit in the payment path at all, and it is exactly the boundary of what
this can do about an atomic breach: the half that can be stated as a number is
refused before the money moves, and the half that needs reading still needs a
round, which is not something anybody would put in front of every payment.
